# the sameness ilk `=`, whose only constructor is `eftbend`.
# matching on `eftbend` replaceth one side of an equation with the other.
show ilk =: [@α: *, a: α, α, *] { eftbend: a = @α a }

## sameness is kept by onwending a fall.
show let leibnitz
  : [@α: *, @β: *, @a: α, @b: α, f: [α, β], eftbend: a = b, (a f) = (b f)]
  ≔ eftbend

## eftwend a sameness soothing.
show let evenmete: [@α: *, @a: α, @b: α, eftbend: a = b, b = a] ≔ eftbend

## yoke two sameness soothings.
show let throughgo
  : [@α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @c: α, eftbend: a = b, q: b = c, a = c]
  ≔ q

## a property of one side holdeth for the other. the property is found by
## abstracting each occurrence of `b` in the expected ilk, or without one,
## each occurrence of `a` in the ilk of the other input.
show let throughbear
  : [@α: *, @a: α, @b: α, @property: [α, *], eftbend: a = b, a property,
    b property]
  ≔ {c ^ c}

## a computed worth with the soothing of what it is, so that a case fall can
## match the worth and still know it: in
## `e inlook { yea, known ^ …, nay, known ^ … }` each case seeth `known`
## as `e = yea` or `e = nay`. the property is found as for `throughbear`.
show let inlook
  : [@α: *, @property: [α, *], a: α, f: [b: α, a = b, b property], a property]
  ≔ a f eftbend

## fall umfanging: a fall that agreeth with another at every input is the same
## as
## it.
## it is a listed assumption, as in agda and lean, where it is not provable
## from sameness unbuilding alone.
show orsetting fall_umfang
  : [@α: *, @β: *, @f: [α, β], @g: [α, β], [a: α, (a f) = (a g)], f = g]
